{
  "image_name": "9f590869e3c4d2326d6b880c41e345a3.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic summary of the key structural and chemical features used to describe porous framework materials. It groups these features into broad categories and shows how different aspects of the framework can vary.\n\nAt the top, the material is divided into intrinsic features, arising from the framework itself, and extrinsic features, represented by an included guest or external species inside the pore. The intrinsic part is further separated into the backbone and the side groups attached to it.\n\nThe center of the diagram emphasizes the pore surface, which is characterized by both pore size and pore structure. Pore size is contrasted as large versus small cavities, while pore structure is shown as either isolated cage-like pores or interconnected channel-type networks.\n\nTwo additional properties branch from the framework description. One is hydrophobicity, illustrated as a spectrum from hydrophilic to moderately hydrophobic and strongly hydrophobic, with blue shading around the framework suggesting the extent of water affinity or exclusion. The other is electronic features, comparing electron-rich and electron-deficient pore environments using red and blue color gradients.\n\nOverall, the figure presents a conceptual map of the main tunable parameters that govern porous material behavior.",
